The root is always at the current end of postorder. After taking it, the cursor moves left. Since that reads the traversal as root, right, left, the recursive function creates the right subtree first.

This version uses the inorder bounds to search only inside the current subtree. It does not copy either input slice and does not change the input.

A single-node tree has `left == right`, creates one node, and both later calls immediately return `nil`.

```go
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * type TreeNode struct {
 *     Data int
 *     Left *TreeNode
 *     Right *TreeNode
 * }
 */

func buildTree(inorder []int, postorder []int) *TreeNode {
    if len(inorder) == 0 || len(postorder) == 0 {
        return nil
    }

    postIndex := len(postorder) - 1

    var build func(int, int) *TreeNode
    build = func(left, right int) *TreeNode {
        if left > right {
            return nil
        }

        value := postorder[postIndex]
        postIndex--
        root := &TreeNode{Data: value}

        middle := left
        for middle <= right && inorder[middle] != value {
            middle++
        }

        root.Right = build(middle+1, right)
        root.Left = build(left, middle-1)
        return root
    }

    return build(0, len(inorder)-1)
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because the current inorder range is searched for every node.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for recursion in a completely skewed tree. No extra array or map is used.

This keeps the array ranges instead of making new subarrays. The final item in each postorder range is the current root. A scan through the matching inorder range gives the size of the left subtree, which is enough to calculate both postorder ranges.

The search is simple, but it can repeat many times on a skewed tree. That makes this version easier to follow than the map version, but slower for large unbalanced trees.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null, right = null }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] inorder, int[] postorder) {
        if (inorder == null || postorder == null || inorder.length == 0) {
            return null;
        }
        return build(inorder, 0, inorder.length - 1,
                     postorder, 0, postorder.length - 1);
    }

    private TreeNode build(int[] inorder, int inLeft, int inRight,
                           int[] postorder, int postLeft, int postRight) {
        if (inLeft > inRight || postLeft > postRight) {
            return null;
        }

        int rootValue = postorder[postRight];
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootValue);

        int rootIndex = inLeft;
        while (rootIndex <= inRight && inorder[rootIndex] != rootValue) {
            rootIndex++;
        }

        int leftSize = rootIndex - inLeft;
        root.left = build(inorder, inLeft, rootIndex - 1,
                          postorder, postLeft, postLeft + leftSize - 1);
        root.right = build(inorder, rootIndex + 1, inRight,
                           postorder, postLeft + leftSize, postRight - 1);

        return root;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because each root may require a long inorder scan.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursive call stack when the tree is skewed. The input arrays stay unchanged.

The last postorder value is the root. Its position in inorder tells where the left side ends and the right side starts. A map saves that position, so every recursive call can find its split quickly.

Because postorder is left, right, root, the pointer moves backward as root, right, left. The right child must be built before the left child.

For `inorder = [9,3,15,20,7]` and `postorder = [9,15,7,20,3]`, the first root is `3`. Its inorder index is `1`, so `9` belongs to the left subtree and the other three values belong to the right subtree. The same split is repeated for each part.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
    unordered_map<int, int> inorderIndex;
    int postIndex;

    TreeNode* build(vector<int>& postorder, int left, int right) {
        if (left > right) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        int value = postorder[postIndex--];
        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(value);
        int middle = inorderIndex[value];

        root->right = build(postorder, middle + 1, right);
        root->left = build(postorder, left, middle - 1);

        return root;
    }

public:
    TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& inorder, vector<int>& postorder) {
        inorderIndex.clear();
        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(inorder.size()); i++) {
            inorderIndex[inorder[i]] = i;
        }

        postIndex = static_cast<int>(postorder.size()) - 1;
        return build(postorder, 0, static_cast<int>(inorder.size()) - 1);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the map and recursion in the worst case. The input arrays are not changed.

## Intuition
So This approach is quite simple

Postorder = Left → Right → Root
👉 So the last element is always the root

Inorder = Left → Root → Right
👉 Root splits the tree into:
Left subtree
Right subtree


In postorder:

Since we process from the end, we must build right subtree first, then left
👉 If you mess this up → wrong tree ❌

🔁 Strategy
Start from the last index of postorder
1) Pick root
2) Find its position in inorder using hashmap
3) Build:
 Right subtree first
 Then left subtree

## Approach
In postorder, the last element is the root. I use inorder to split the tree into left and right subtrees. Since I process postorder from the end, I construct the right subtree first, followed by the left. A hashmap helps me locate root positions in O(1), making the overall complexity O(n).

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    unordered_map<int, int> mp;

    TreeNode* build(vector<int>& post, int& idx, int l, int r) {
        if (l > r) return nullptr;

        // Root from postorder
        TreeNode* root = new TreeNode(post[idx]);
        int mid = mp[post[idx]];
        idx--;

        // IMPORTANT: build right first
        root->right = build(post, idx, mid + 1, r);
        root->left = build(post, idx, l, mid - 1);

        return root;
    }

    TreeNode* buildTree(vector<int>& inorder, vector<int>& postorder) {
        // Store inorder indices
        for (int i = 0; i < inorder.size(); i++) {
            mp[inorder[i]] = i;
        }

        int idx = postorder.size() - 1;
        return build(postorder, idx, 0, inorder.size() - 1);
    }
};
```

